<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- HTTPS to api.weather.gov, the NEXRAD archive, and basemap tiles. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    <!-- LocationHelper's requestLocationUpdates() subscription, and the
         getLastKnownLocation() poll on the GPS thread in rustdar's android
         location module that it feeds. Both are requested at runtime together;
         COARSE alone (the "approximate only" dialog answer) still serves the
         network provider. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
      WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE and READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE used to be declared here,
      carried over verbatim from the cargo-apk metadata. Nothing in the app has
      ever touched external storage: the zone cache goes to getCacheDir() and the
      UI config to the internal data path, both derived from
      AndroidApp::internal_data_path() in android_main, and neither needs a
      permission. Declaring them cost a line in the Play listing and on the
      install screen for a capability the app does not use.
    -->

    <application
        android:label="Rustdar"
        android:icon="@android:drawable/sym_def_app_icon"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault.NoActionBar.Fullscreen"

        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"

        android:hasCode="true">

        <!--
          android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback is deliberately ABSENT, and this
          comment is the measurement that keeps it absent.

          Setting it to "true" opts the app into the predictive-back dispatcher
          on API 33+, which is what would let com.rustdar.BackHandler decline a
          press and let the platform draw its own back-to-home preview. It was
          tried, on a device: with the attribute present, an unclaimed press
          does exactly what the design wanted (the launcher takes the foreground,
          type = CLOSE, no moveTaskToBack) and THE APP CAN THEN NEVER BE
          REOPENED. Relaunching starts a fresh android.app.NativeActivity, and
          android_main does not run for a second Activity in this process, so
          the stock splash stays up forever. Measured as a paired A/B on one
          build one attribute apart, 2/2 on each arm (WO-RP-SPIKE leg 4b,
          2026-08-20); with the attribute absent the same press backgrounds the
          app and it resumes with its cached radar image.

          So back is delivered the legacy way on every API level this app runs
          on: KEYCODE_BACK through the native input queue into
          InputHandler::take_back_out_press. BackHandler's callback is
          registered and unregistered truthfully by the claim Rust pushes, and
          the platform never invokes it — inert, exactly as the Java class this
          replaced was.

          What has to be true before this attribute comes back: android_main
          must run for a re-created Activity. Until then the attribute is the
          one thing standing between a working app and an unopenable one, and
          from targetSdk 36 there is no opting out — so that fix is the
          prerequisite for the bump, not a nicety.
        -->

        <!--
          allowBackup is false, and that is a downgrade from the "true" this
          inherited from cargo-apk. With no fullBackupContent or
          dataExtractionRules to scope it, "true" means Android uploads the whole
          internal data directory to the user's Google Backup: the UI config, and
          the zone-geometry cache under getCacheDir(). None of it is worth
          restoring — the cache is re-fetched on demand and the config is a
          handful of unit preferences — and the last-known-location handling
          means the data is more sensitive than it is useful.

          If backup is wanted later, add it deliberately: android:allowBackup
          plus an android:dataExtractionRules (API 31+) and
          android:fullBackupContent (below 31) that name the config and exclude
          the cache. Turning the flag back on without those is what this comment
          exists to prevent.
        -->

        <!--
          android:networkSecurityConfig is the entire reason this build moved off
          cargo-apk. See res/xml/network_security_config.xml for the full rationale;
          in short, Android's TrustManager fetches api.weather.gov's CRL over plain
          HTTP (Let's Encrypt R13 publishes no OCSP responder at all), and that fetch
          is subject to this app's own cleartext policy. Blocking cleartext app-wide
          therefore breaks HTTPS to weather.gov rather than hardening it.

          The attribute value is a *resource reference*: aapt2 must compile that XML
          into resources.arsc and assign it an ID. That is what cargo-apk could not
          do, and why injecting the file into the APK zip was never going to work.

          Deliberately NOT set here: android:usesCleartextTraffic. Android ignores
          that flag whenever a networkSecurityConfig is present, so spelling it
          "false" would read like a guarantee the platform is not honouring. The
          config file is the single source of truth: cleartext permitted in
          base-config so the platform's own revocation fetches succeed, and denied
          per-domain for every host rustdar itself talks to.
        -->

        <activity
            android:name="android.app.NativeActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"

            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|screenSize|screenLayout|smallestScreenSize|density|uiMode">

            <!--
              uiMode in that list is load-bearing and easy to drop as "cosmetic".
              Without it a dark/light switch — and, on a foldable, an unfold —
              tears down and recreates the Activity underneath a running native
              event loop, which deadlocks and ANRs instead of relaunching cleanly.
              The Rust side handles all of these in-app: theme via the polling
              thread in the rustdar crate, size via the normal resize path.
            -->

            <!-- NativeActivity dlsym()s android_main out of this .so. The name is
                 the rustdar crate's [lib] name, i.e. librustdar_native.so. -->
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.lib_name"
                android:value="rustdar_native" />

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
